        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(agents): normalize surrogate cache fingerprints",
        "body": "﻿Closes #100957\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where prompt-cache/cache-trace fingerprinting could preserve malformed UTF-16 surrogate halves when serializing JSON-like diagnostic inputs, causing otherwise equivalent cache boundary payloads to hash differently when malformed external text is present.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n`stableStringify` now normalizes string values and object keys with the existing provider surrogate sanitizer from `@openclaw/ai/internal/runtime`, so cache keys, cache trace digests, and diagnostics share the same malformed-surrogate behavior as provider outbound text. The transport sanitizer now reuses the same implementation while keeping its previous nullish-input guard.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers and operators get more stable prompt-cache fingerprints when malformed Unicode reaches cache trace inputs. Valid surrogate pairs such as emoji are preserved, while unpaired high and low surrogate halves are removed before deterministic stringification.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `git diff --check` passed.\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/stable-stringify.test.ts src/agents/transport-stream-shared.test.ts` passed: `stable-stringify.test.ts` 5 tests, `transport-stream-shared.test.ts` 10 tests.\n- Related open PR search for `100957 surrogate cache` returned no open matches.\n\nKnown proof gap:\n\n- `.agents/skills/autoreview/scripts/autoreview --mode branch --base upstream/main` was attempted with UTF-8 env settings, but the local Codex engine failed before producing structured findings because the configured OpenAI API key returned `401 invalid_api_key`.\r\n",
